### Sample 10 - Container Apps scale-out
###
### Replace @app with the FQDN deploy.ps1 printed.
###
### The proof in this sample is NOT in the responses below - it is in the Verifier terminal and
### in Log Analytics. Keep the verifier running while you work through this file:
###
###   cd Verifier
###   dotnet run --drain true          # empty the subscription first
###   dotnet run --idle 45 --expect 100

@app = https://ca-outbox-scaleout.grayriver-342fc9fb.southeastasia.azurecontainerapps.io

### 1. Which replica answered? Send this a few times - ingress round-robins between replicas.
### This proves the ingress is balanced. It does NOT prove both dispatchers are working;
### README section 6 has the Log Analytics query that does.
GET {{app}}/node

### 2. One order. Note the outboxId in the response.
POST {{app}}/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenantId": "dnv-energy",
  "customerId": "cust-1001",
  "total": 149.50
}

### 3. Inspect it. Within a few seconds status is Delivered and attemptCount is 1.
### claimOwner is null - CompleteAsync clears it, so a delivered row does not remember
### which replica sent it.
GET {{app}}/outbox/01a1ceef-f884-79ae-a470-5921378c393e

### 4. The burst. 100 orders through one request, returning all 100 outbox ids.
### The verifier must report: 100 received, 100 distinct, 0 duplicated.
POST {{app}}/orders/bulk?count=100

### 5. Counters. delivered grows by 100; pending and failed return to 0.
GET {{app}}/outbox/stats

### 6. Prove the verifier can actually fail.
###
### Start a fresh verifier (dotnet run --idle 30), then send this twice with the SAME id.
### /resend calls the destination's SendAsync with the stored record - byte for byte what the
### dispatcher does on a retry. The scaleout-events topic has duplicate detection switched OFF
### on purpose, so both replays arrive and the verifier reports:
###
###   messages received : 2
###   distinct ids      : 1
###   duplicated ids    : 1
###   FAIL - the same outbox record was delivered more than once.
POST {{app}}/outbox/01a1ceef-f884-79ae-a470-5921378c393e/resend

### 7. Again. Same id, same result - the duplicate count goes to 2.
POST {{app}}/outbox/01a1ceef-f884-79ae-a470-5921378c393e/resend

### 8. The kill test. Run the verifier with --idle 90 (NOT 45), fire this, then restart the
### revision from PowerShell while it is still dispatching:
###
###   $rev = az containerapp show -g rg-outbox-samples -n ca-outbox-scaleout `
###       --only-show-errors --query properties.latestRevisionName -o tsv
###   az containerapp revision restart -g rg-outbox-samples -n ca-outbox-scaleout `
###       --revision $rev --only-show-errors
###
### Still 100 received, 100 distinct, 0 duplicated. The 90-second window matters: rows claimed
### by the dead replica are invisible to the survivor until the 60-second claim expires.
POST {{app}}/orders/bulk?count=100

### 9. Index.
GET {{app}}/
